module bindings.python.pathlib_containment_check_scope;

// pathlib documents two containment operations: PurePath.relative_to(other)
// raises ValueError when the receiver is not below other, and
// PurePath.is_relative_to(other) answers the same question as a bool. Both are
// purely lexical -- PurePath keeps ".." segments verbatim -- so the documented
// confinement idiom canonicalizes first and compares the resolved forms, which
// is why the canonicalizer and the comparison are read together here.
//
// The scope is the enclosing function rather than the call, because the raising
// form is written as a bare statement inside a try block whose handler turns the
// ValueError into a rejection. A statement in an exception region is not on the
// straight-line path to the code that follows the try, so a call-anchored
// control never reaches the file access it protects; the function is the
// granularity at which this idiom is visible at all. Python context call paths
// are function-confined, so a canonicalizer or a comparison written in a sibling
// function is not credited.
//
// Residual, in the false-negative direction: relative_to also computes a display
// path relative to a known root, and a function that does that while touching
// files elsewhere reads the same way. Requiring the canonicalizer keeps the
// plain lexical uses out, but it cannot tell a discarded result from a consumed
// one.
binding pathlibContainmentCheckScope {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "python"
    and node.context.callPath contains "relative_to"
    and containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["resolve", "realpath", "abspath", "normpath"])
  emit check core.PathAccessCheck at node {
    covers endpoint {
      anchor: node
    }
  }
}
